1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,540
In the 1690s,

2
00:00:01,540 --> 00:00:02,800
a girl named Jane told

3
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:03,860
a court about the night

4
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:05,640
her mother was killed.

5
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:06,920
She said they had just woken

6
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:09,100
from their “first sleep.”

7
00:00:09,100 --> 00:00:10,280
Centuries later,

8
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:11,480
a historian read that,

9
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:13,120
and stopped.

10
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:14,160
First sleep?

11
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:15,720
Then what was the second?

12
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:17,700
He found both in diaries,

13
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:19,460
plays and doctors' books.

14
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:21,640
About two thousand times.

15
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:22,780
Many Europeans once

16
00:00:22,780 --> 00:00:24,140
slept twice a night,

17
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:25,080
with an hour or so

18
00:00:25,080 --> 00:00:26,620
awake around midnight.

19
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:27,620
In that hour,

20
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:28,660
people prayed,

21
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:29,340
talked,

22
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:30,820
or visited the neighbours.

23
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:32,340
But as lamps got brighter,

24
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,060
the two sleeps became one,

25
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:34,600
and the phrase

26
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:35,860
was forgotten.

27
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:37,040
In one experiment,

28
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,460
volunteers kept in darkness

29
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:40,200
fourteen hours a night began

30
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:42,310
sleeping in two halves.

31
00:00:42,340 --> 00:00:43,480
Researchers still debate

32
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,900
how common two sleeps were.

33
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:47,120
Jane never explained what

34
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,000
“first sleep” meant.

35
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:50,360
She didn't need to.

36
00:00:50,360 --> 00:00:51,140
Back then,

37
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:52,500
everyone knew.
